---
import { Text } from "@datadog/druids/typography/Text";
import ConceptPager from "../../components/ConceptPager.astro";
import Diagram from "../../components/Diagram.astro";
import Rich from "../../components/Rich.astro";
import type { FlowEdge, FlowNode } from "../../components/FlowDiagram.tsx";
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import { href } from "../../lib/site";

const oneDbNodes: FlowNode[] = [
  { id: "app", label: "Storefront", sub: "the web shop", x: 0, y: 100, tone: "app", detail: { text: "Writes each order straight into the database as part of checkout. Simple, and correct for a small shop." } },
  { id: "db", label: "Orders database", sub: "PostgreSQL or SQL Server", x: 260, y: 100, tone: "store", width: 200, detail: { text: "Everything reads and writes here: checkout, reports, exports and other services. Every new reader adds load to the database that takes orders, and a slow report can slow checkout." } },
  { id: "dash", label: "Dashboards", sub: "scan the live tables", x: 560, y: 0, tone: "serve", detail: { text: "Analytical queries read months of orders at once. On a row-oriented database tuned for checkout, they're slow and they compete with the app for CPU, memory and locks." } },
  { id: "finance", label: "Finance export", sub: "nightly CSV", x: 560, y: 110, tone: "serve", detail: { text: "Each export is custom code against the app's tables. Rename a column for the app and the export breaks, usually discovered at month end." } },
  { id: "email", label: "Email service", sub: "polls for new orders", x: 560, y: 220, tone: "app", detail: { text: "Polling the orders table every few seconds is more load, and couples the email service to the storefront's schema." } },
];
const oneDbEdges: FlowEdge[] = [
  { from: "app", to: "db", label: "writes" },
  { from: "db", to: "dash", label: "heavy scans" },
  { from: "db", to: "finance", label: "exports" },
  { from: "db", to: "email", label: "polling" },
];

const pipeNodes: FlowNode[] = [
  { id: "app", label: "Storefront", sub: "publishes order events", x: 0, y: 0, tone: "app", detail: { text: "Checkout publishes one event per order and moves on. The storefront doesn't know or care who reads it, and keeps taking orders when a reader is down." } },
  { id: "topic", label: "orders topic", sub: "event stream", x: 250, y: 0, tone: "stream", detail: { text: "Holds every order event for a retention period. Each reader keeps its own position, so a new reader can start from the past, and a broken one can catch up after it's fixed." } },
  { id: "proc", label: "Order processor", sub: "validates, batches", x: 500, y: 0, tone: "process", detail: { text: "Turns the stream into files every few minutes. Commits its position only after a file is written, so a crash replays a batch instead of losing it." } },
  { id: "lake", label: "Landing zone", sub: "files by date and hour", x: 750, y: 0, tone: "store", detail: { text: "Raw files in cheap storage, kept as they arrived. If a transformation turns out to be wrong, the warehouse can be rebuilt from here." } },
  { id: "dw", label: "Warehouse", sub: "raw → staging → marts", x: 750, y: 170, tone: "store", width: 190, detail: { text: "A separate analytical store, built every 15 minutes by the orchestrator. Heavy queries run here, never on the storefront's database." } },
  { id: "bi", label: "Dashboards", sub: "read the marts", x: 500, y: 170, tone: "serve", detail: { text: "Read clean, modeled tables with agreed definitions, instead of reverse-engineering the app's schema." } },
  { id: "email", label: "Email service", sub: "its own subscription", x: 250, y: 170, tone: "muted", detail: { text: "Another reader of the same events, with its own position. Adding it doesn't touch the storefront or the warehouse. (Illustrative: not in this demo.)" } },
  { id: "fraud", label: "Fraud check", sub: "reacts in seconds", x: 0, y: 170, tone: "muted", detail: { text: "Streaming lets some readers react in real time while the warehouse loads in batches. (Illustrative: not in this demo.)" } },
];
const pipeEdges: FlowEdge[] = [
  { from: "app", to: "topic", label: "per order" },
  { from: "topic", to: "proc" },
  { from: "proc", to: "lake", label: "every 5 min" },
  { from: "lake", to: "dw", label: "every 15 min", fromSide: "bottom", toSide: "top" },
  { from: "dw", to: "bi", fromSide: "left", toSide: "right" },
  { from: "topic", to: "email", dashed: true, fromSide: "bottom", toSide: "top" },
  { from: "topic", to: "fraud", dashed: true, fromSide: "bottom", toSide: "top" },
];
---

<Base
  title="Why build a pipeline like this?"
  description="One database compared with a delegated, event-driven pipeline: what the moving parts buy, and what they cost."
>
  <a class="back-link" href={href("concepts/")}>← All concepts</a>
  <article class="concept">
    <header class="hero hero-compact">
      <Text as="p" size="sm" variant="tertiary" weight="bold" textTransform="uppercase">Concept 1 of 3</Text>
      <Text as="h1" size="xxl" weight="bold">Why build a pipeline like this?</Text>
      <Text as="p" size="lg" variant="secondary" className="lede">
        Contoso could write every order into one database and point the dashboards at it. Many companies start there,
        and some should stay. This lesson is about what changes as a business grows, and why the demo's pipeline looks
        the way it does.
      </Text>
    </header>

    <section class="lesson">
      <Text as="h2" size="lg" weight="bold">The simple version: one database for everything</Text>
      <Text as="p">
        The storefront writes orders into its database, and everything else reads from the same place. Select a box to see
        where the strain shows up.
      </Text>
      <Diagram nodes={oneDbNodes} edges={oneDbEdges} height={320} initial="db" />
      <Text as="p">
        This works until it doesn't. The database that takes orders is tuned for many small, fast transactions (OLTP). A
        dashboard that sums a year of orders wants the opposite: big scans and aggregates (OLAP). Put both on one server
        and the reports slow checkout, every new consumer is coupled to the app's tables, and there's no record of what
        the data looked like before the last bad update.
      </Text>
    </section>

    <section class="lesson">
      <Text as="h2" size="lg" weight="bold">The delegated version: each step has one job and one owner</Text>
      <Text as="p">
        This is the demo's shape. The storefront announces each order as an event; independent readers take it from there.
        Dashed boxes are readers a company might add later; they aren't part of the demo.
      </Text>
      <Diagram nodes={pipeNodes} edges={pipeEdges} height={340} initial="topic" />
    </section>

    <section class="lesson">
      <Text as="h2" size="lg" weight="bold">What the extra parts buy</Text>
      <dl class="benefits">
        <dt>The app stays fast</dt>
        <dd>Analytical queries run on a separate warehouse built for them, never on the database that takes orders.</dd>
        <dt>Producers and consumers are decoupled</dt>
        <dd>
          The storefront publishes once. Adding a reader (email, fraud, a new warehouse) means a new subscription, not a
          change to the storefront, and a reader that's down doesn't stop checkout.
        </dd>
        <dt>Nothing is lost, and anything can be replayed</dt>
        <dd>
          The stream keeps events for a retention period, and the landing zone keeps every raw file. Fix a bug in the
          transformation and rebuild from the raw data; nobody has to ask the storefront to resend.
        </dd>
        <dt>Each team owns one step</dt>
        <dd>
          Dave owns the order event, Emily the loads, Rajesh the schemas, Priya the business logic, John the reports.
          Each hands off through a contract (a message shape, a table, a column's meaning), so teams can change their own
          step without coordinating every release.
        </dd>
        <dt>Each step scales on its own</dt>
        <dd>
          Storage is cheap and separate from compute. A slow mart build gets more compute without touching ingestion, and
          a traffic spike buffers in the stream instead of overwhelming the warehouse.
        </dd>
        <dt>Real time where it matters, batch where it's cheaper</dt>
        <dd>
          The same events can feed a fraud check in seconds and the warehouse every 15 minutes.
        </dd>
      </dl>
    </section>

    <section class="lesson">
      <Text as="h2" size="lg" weight="bold">What they cost</Text>
      <ul class="ticks">
        <li><Rich text="More moving parts, run by more people: a stream, a processor, storage, an orchestrator, a warehouse and a BI tool, each of which can fail on its own." /></li>
        <li><Rich text="Latency: the demo's dashboards are up to about 20 minutes behind checkout, where the one-database version was live." /></li>
        <li><Rich text="Failures get quieter. A run can succeed and load nothing, a schema change two steps upstream can break a report, and a logic bug can produce plausible wrong numbers." /></li>
        <li><Rich text="Contracts need care. A field the storefront drops shows up as nulls in a mart a day later, owned by someone else." /></li>
      </ul>
      <div class="callout">
        <Text as="p" weight="bold">This is where observability comes in.</Text>
        <Text as="p">
          The delegated model trades one obvious failure (the database is slow) for many quiet ones spread across teams.
          Each persona module follows one of them: Datadog has to show which step broke, whose step it is, and what it
          reaches downstream. See the <a href={href()}>persona modules</a>.
        </Text>
      </div>
      <Text as="p" size="sm" variant="secondary">
        A rule of thumb: start with one database and a read replica for reports. Move to a pipeline when reports slow the
        app, when several teams need the same data in different shapes, or when you need history and replay.
      </Text>
    </section>

    <ConceptPager slug="why-pipelines" />
  </article>
</Base>
